{
  "requirement": "When authoring evidence for a requirement, its complete fenced declaration MUST\nbe included in the mandatory input set defined by\n{cite}`requirements.authoring.mandatory_inputs`.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Implementation.** `AssessmentSources::prepare` in `src/authoring/inputs.rs` (lines 53–170) always starts its selector list with `InputSelector::Requirements { requirements: subject_name }`. That is the subject's own declaration, and it comes before any referenced subjects, source references, existing-evidence selectors or terms are added.\n\n- `source_input` resolves that selector against the loaded declarations, so the complete fenced `:::{req}` block is included. The subject selector is never dropped: `generated_evidence_selector` returns `false` for `Requirements` selectors, so it cannot be filtered out as generated evidence.\n- `prepare` rejects unknown subjects with an error, so a subject whose declaration cannot be found is reported before any model inference.\n- The subject's declaration is part of the set `mandatory_inputs()` returns. It is stored in `Self.mandatory`, which is the initial bundle.\n\n**Tests.**\n- `bundle_combines_direct_references_terms_and_current_existing_selectors` is cited with `{test}` for this requirement. For `requirements.ready` it checks the whole bundle: the cited impl and test functions, the referencing example `requirements.case`, the cited `requirements.base`, the exclusion of the distant requirement, exactly two definitions, refreshed file content, and that the old judgment is omitted.\n- Its last assertion calls `prepare` on the example `requirements.case` and checks that `mandatory_inputs()[0][\"requirements\"] == \"requirements.case\"`. That confirms the subject's own declaration is the first mandatory input. The requirement path uses the same code, so it is covered too.\n- `mandatory_git_inputs_require_staging_declarations_sources_terms_and_old_selectors` is also cited. It shows that the declaration file (`md/requirements.md`) must be readable or staged, and that failure is reported up front. This covers the \"unresolvable mandatory inputs\" aspect.\n\n**Minor gap.** No assertion checks that `requirements.ready` itself appears first in the requirement case, or that its text is the full fenced block. The example-subject assertion exercises the same code path, and `src/authoring/mod.rs` tests that the prompt contains `requirements.ready`. I judge this adequate, with no acknowledged FIXME.",
